---
import DocsLayout from '@/layouts/DocsLayout.astro';
import { DocCards, Callout, DocsTable } from '@/molecules/DocComponents/DocComponents';

const CARDS = [
  { icon: '🏛️', title: 'Architecture-First Development', desc: 'Why OpenFlows plans before it executes, and why PLAN.md and CONTRACT.md are the real deliverables.', href: '/docs/concepts/architecture-first' },
  { icon: '👥', title: 'The Agent Team', desc: 'NEXUS, FORGE, SENTINEL, VESSEL, and LORE  -  their roles, boundaries, and the issue-to-merge pipeline.', href: '/docs/concepts/agent-team' },
  { icon: '🧠', title: 'PocketFlow & SharedStore', desc: 'The directed flow graph, the Node lifecycle, and typed Redis state contracts that keep the system coherent.', href: '/docs/concepts/pocketflow-sharedstore' },
  { icon: '🔗', title: 'Coder Integration', desc: 'How OpenFlows runs as a thin orchestration brain on top of a self-hosted Coder deployment.', href: '/docs/concepts/coder-integration' },
  { icon: '🛡️', title: 'Security Model', desc: 'Identity, audit, secrets, ephemeral workspaces, and the no-PAT, no-LLM-keys-in-workspaces policy.', href: '/docs/concepts/security-model' },
];

const CONCEPT_ROWS = [
  ['Architecture-First', 'Boilerplate is cheap; correctness is not. Agents write code only after a human-reviewable plan and contract exist.', '/docs/concepts/architecture-first'],
  ['Agent Team', 'Specialised roles with narrow boundaries prevent a single agent from drifting into both authorship and review.', '/docs/concepts/agent-team'],
  ['PocketFlow & SharedStore', 'A deterministic graph and a typed state bus let the orchestrator reason about progress, not just emit prompts.', '/docs/concepts/pocketflow-sharedstore'],
  ['Coder Integration', 'Coder is the runtime. OpenFlows does not provision VMs, manage SSH keys, or host AI models itself.', '/docs/concepts/coder-integration'],
  ['Security Model', 'Secrets and identity are handled by Coder; workers start empty and are destroyed after merge.', '/docs/concepts/security-model'],
];
---

<DocsLayout breadcrumbs={[{ label: 'Docs', href: '/docs' }, { label: 'Core Concepts' }]}>
  <h1>Core Concepts</h1>
  <p>
    OpenFlows is not a model, an IDE, or a CI/CD product. It is an <strong>orchestration layer</strong>
    that turns a self-hosted Coder deployment into an autonomous engineering team. The concepts in this
    section explain the ideas that make that possible: why planning beats prompting, why five narrow agents
    beat one general-purpose agent, how a directed graph and a typed store keep the system coherent, and
    why security is a property of the runtime rather than a policy document.
  </p>
  <p>
    If you are a developer or operator trying to decide whether OpenFlows fits your organisation, read
    these pages first. If you are already running the system, they explain the reasoning behind the
    configuration files, the registry schema, and the workspace lifecycle you see in production.
  </p>

  <Callout type="info" title="Architecture is the product">
    The central thesis of OpenFlows is that the hard part of software engineering is the architecture,
    the contract, and the review gate  -  not the code itself. When agents are cheap and models can write
    boilerplate in seconds, the value shifts from keystrokes to specification. OpenFlows encodes that
    shift into a repeatable pipeline.
  </Callout>

  <h2>Concepts at a Glance</h2>
  <DocsTable
    headers={['Concept', 'What problem it solves', 'Read more']}
    rows={CONCEPT_ROWS.map(([concept, desc, href]) => [
      `<strong>${concept}</strong>`,
      desc,
      `<a href="${href}">Details →</a>`,
    ])}
  />

  <h2>How the Concepts Fit Together</h2>
  <p>
    The five concepts form a stack. At the bottom, <strong>Coder</strong> provides the runtime: identity,
    workspaces, model governance, and audit. On top of that runtime, OpenFlows runs a <strong>PocketFlow</strong>
    graph whose nodes are the five agents. The agents communicate through a <strong>SharedStore</strong> with
    typed contracts, and the whole flow is governed by the <strong>architecture-first</strong> rule that no
    implementation begins until a plan has been written and reviewed. Finally, the <strong>security model</strong>
    makes those guarantees concrete by ensuring that no worker workspace ever contains secrets, credentials,
    or long-lived state.
  </p>
  <ol>
    <li>
      <strong>Plan:</strong> a GitHub issue is interpreted as a ticket. NEXUS assigns it to FORGE, which writes
      <code>PLAN.md</code>. SENTINEL writes <code>CONTRACT.md</code>  -  a reviewable contract, not a guess.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>Execute:</strong> FORGE implements the plan in segments inside an ephemeral Coder workspace.
      SENTINEL evaluates each segment and produces <code>segment-N-eval.md</code>.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>Merge:</strong> after a final review, FORGE opens a PR. VESSEL monitors CI and squash-merges.
      LORE documents the change, and the workspace is destroyed.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>Recover:</strong> NEXUS continuously reconciles the SharedStore. If a node crashes, an agent
      hallucinates, or a workspace disappears, the flow resumes at the correct phase.
    </li>
  </ol>

  <h2>Where to Start</h2>
  <DocCards cards={CARDS} />

  <Callout type="tip" title="Read these pages before editing registry.json">
    The <code>orchestration/agent/registry.json</code> file makes a lot more sense once you understand the
    agent-team model and the Coder integration. If you are tempted to change a model or disable an agent,
    read the corresponding concept page first.
  </Callout>
</DocsLayout>
